To solve the problem of selecting a president, vice-president, and treasurer from a math club with 20 members, we need to understand that each position is distinct and that no member can hold more than one office. This is a permutation problem because the order in which we select the individuals matters (i.e., selecting Alice as president and Bob as vice-president is different from selecting Bob as president and Alice as vice-president).

