Find the value of $ k $ such that the lines are coplanar.

To determine the value of $ k $ such that the two lines are coplanar, we start by analyzing the general condition for coplanarity of two lines in 3D space. Two lines are coplanar if the vector connecting any two points on the lines, together with the direction vectors of the lines, is linearly dependent. This is equivalent to the scalar triple product of these vectors being zero.

### Step 1: Parametrize the lines
We are given two lines in parametric form:

- **Line 1:** 
  $$
  \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -3 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix} + t \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ k \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}
  $$
  This line passes through point $ \mathbf{P}_1 = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -3 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix} $ and has direction vector $ \vec{v}_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ k \\ 7 \end{pmatrix} $.

- **Line 2:**
  $$
  \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 4 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix} + u \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 4 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}
  $$
  This line passes through point $ \mathbf{P}_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 4 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix} $ and has direction vector $ \vec{v}_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 4 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix} $.

### Step 2: Find the vector connecting the two points
The vector connecting a point on Line 1 to a point on Line 2 is:
$$
\vec{P}_1\vec{P}_2 = \mathbf{P}_2 - \mathbf{P}_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 2 - (-1) \\ 4 - (-3) \\ 6 - (-5) \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 7 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix}
$$

### Step 3: Compute the scalar triple product
The scalar triple product of $ \vec{P}_1\vec{P}_2 $, $ \vec{v}_1 $, and $ \vec{v}_2 $ is given by:
$$
\vec{P}_1\vec{P}_2 \cdot (\vec{v}_1 \times \vec{v}_2) = 0
$$

First, compute the cross product $ \vec{v}_1 \times \vec{v}_2 $:
$$
\vec{v}_1 \times \vec{v}_2 = \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
3 & k & 7 \\
1 & 4 & 7
\end{vmatrix}
= \mathbf{i}(k \cdot 7 - 7 \cdot 4) - \mathbf{j}(3 \cdot 7 - 7 \cdot 1) + \mathbf{k}(3 \cdot 4 - k \cdot 1)
$$
$$
= \mathbf{i}(7k - 28) - \mathbf{j}(21 - 7) + \mathbf{k}(12 - k)
$$
$$
= \begin{pmatrix} 7k - 28 \\ -14 \\ 12 - k \end{pmatrix}
$$

Now take the dot product of this with $ \vec{P}_1\vec{P}_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 7 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix} $:
$$
\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 7 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 7k - 28 \\ -14 \\ 12 - k \end{pmatrix}
= 3(7k - 28) + 7(-14) + 11(12 - k)
$$

### Step 4: Simplify and solve for $ k $
$$
= 21k - 84 - 98 + 132 - 11k
$$
$$
= (21k - 11k) + (-84 - 98 + 132)
$$
$$
= 10k - 50
$$

Set the scalar triple product to zero:
$$
10k - 50 = 0
$$
$$
10k = 50
$$
$$
k = \boxed{5}
$$
Thus, the value of $ k $ that makes the lines coplanar is $ \boxed{5} $. The lines are coplanar when $ k = 5 $.
